=== HYPERPARAMETERS ===

Any parameter name that is NOT in the canonical set must have a literal
default value. These are tunable hyperparameters: ``eps``, ``alpha``,
``depth``, ``quantile``, ``lookback``, etc. The runtime introspects
``__init__`` and passes them through. Per-task overrides flow on top via
the catalog's params block.

If you need a per-task CONSTANT (e.g. ``initial_capital``,
``risk_free_rate``) that is not in the canonical set yet, give it a
sensible default for now (e.g. ``initial_capital=1.0``) and the metric
will be portable across tasks. We are tracking this gap separately.

=== REQUIREMENTS ===

- Output valid Python code only. Start with: ``from reward_bank import Loss``.
- Do NOT redefine the Loss base class.
- Conform to RULES R1-R6 above.
- Choose the SINGLE most appropriate shape (forecasting / generative /
  embedding-pair / single-fake / multi-sample).
- Never accept a whole ``nn.Module`` (model or discriminator) as an init
  argument — use the embedding-pair pattern with ``real_emb`` /
  ``fake_emb`` if you need feature-space distances.
- Handle edge cases (division by zero, empty tensors) gracefully.
- Do NOT define a standalone evaluate() function. Only define a single
  Loss subclass.
- Do NOT add ``**kwargs`` (or ``*args``) to ``__init__`` — list every
  parameter explicitly. The Loss base only takes ``name``, so
  ``super().__init__(name=name)`` is sufficient.
- The metric MUST visibly depend on ``pred`` / ``fake_emb`` /
  ``pred_samples`` (whichever your shape uses). A metric whose result is
  computed purely from gt is broken — the agent's score wouldn't depend on
  what they predicted.

